latest_plan = "Based on the reflection, here's a detailed step-by-step plan to solve the challenge:\n\n1. Scan the input grid:\n   - Iterate through each cell in the grid from top to bottom, left to right.\n\n2. Identify shapes:\n   - When a non-black cell is found, use a flood fill algorithm to identify all connected cells of the same color.\n   - Keep track of visited cells to avoid processing the same shape multiple times.\n\n3. Extract shapes:\n   - For each identified shape:\n     a. Determine the minimal bounding box that contains all cells of the shape.\n     b. Extract this bounding box as a new grid, preserving the shape's structure.\n     c. Store this extracted shape along with its original top-left position in the input grid.\n\n4. Sort shapes:\n   - Sort the extracted shapes based on their original positions in the input grid:\n     - Primary sort key: row number (top to bottom)\n     - Secondary sort key: column number (left to right)\n\n5. Determine output orientation:\n   - Count the number of extracted shapes.\n   - If the count is greater than 2, set orientation to vertical; otherwise, set it to horizontal.\n\n6. Calculate output grid dimensions:\n   - For vertical orientation:\n     - Width = maximum width among all extracted shapes\n     - Height = sum of heights of all extracted shapes\n   - For horizontal orientation:\n     - Width = sum of widths of all extracted shapes\n     - Height = maximum height among all extracted shapes\n\n7. Create output grid:\n   - Initialize a new ColoredGrid with the calculated dimensions, filled with black (0) cells.\n\n8. Place shapes in output grid:\n   - For vertical orientation:\n     - Place each shape at the top of the next available row.\n     - Center the shape horizontally within its row.\n     - Fill any remaining cells in the row with black (0).\n   - For horizontal orientation:\n     - Place shapes side by side, starting from the left.\n     - Center each shape vertically within the grid height.\n     - Do not add any spacing between shapes.\n\n9. Return the output grid.\n\nThis plan takes into account the key observations from our reflection:\n- It extracts shapes in their minimal bounding box rather than forcing a 3x3 grid.\n- It determines the orientation based on the number of shapes.\n- It arranges shapes vertically for more than two shapes, and horizontally for two or fewer.\n- It ensures proper spacing (or lack thereof) between shapes based on the orientation.\n- It preserves the order of shapes as they appear in the input grid.\n\nThis approach should work consistently for all the given examples and test cases, addressing the nuances we identified in our reflection."
